    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_TOOL_INVOCATION">
      The SDD mechanical tools are `gennady` CLI commands. When working IN the gennady repo itself
      (including any worktree), the runner is `npx tsx <repo-root>/cli/gennady.ts`; in every other
      project it is `npx gennady`. Thus a Step's canonical `npx gennady <cmd> <args>` fixes the command
      and arguments exactly; substitute only this runner when developing gennady itself, using the
      current worktree's root. Tools: `sdd-state`, `sdd-task`, `sdd-extract`, `sdd-verify`, `sdd-log`,
      `sdd-sync`, `sdd-check`, `sdd-new`, `sdd-migrate`, `sdd-orient`, `sdd-session`, `orient`, `lint`, `yagni`, `testcov`. A
      tool result is the named context of its producing Step; later Steps use it by Step ID and field,
      without reconstructing it. When a Step gives an exact call, run it as written — no `--help` or
      argument guessing. Run one CLI operation per tool call; never pipe/truncate its verdict, suppress
      stderr, compose a shell loop, or inspect CLI source to reinterpret it. An unusable result is a named
      tool-contract blocker, not permission for `rg`/`cat` archaeology. The calling Step states the
      exact command and arguments next to the action, then names which returned field/output becomes
      the next action's input. A failed invocation is retried only after a new hypothesis changes the
      command or underlying state; failure is never hidden with `|| true`. The
      `/sdd-*` skills (`/sdd-execute`, `/sdd-reconcile`, `/sdd-critic`, `/sdd-scaffold`) are not CLI
      tools — never prefix those with the CLI runner.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE" cross-cutting="true">
      The document's language is a few-shot for the agent's own speech: operator-facing Russian must
      read like a senior engineer talking to a colleague — plain, direct, concrete. Sounding smart or
      sharp is the failure mode, not the goal. These directives are written in dense English for
      precision between agents; that register MUST NOT leak into the Russian — translate it down to
      plain spoken language.

      Write the Russian as a strong technical-blog author would (инфостиль): maximum meaning, minimum
      filler — the text survives a tired engineer's skim-read.

      Hard bans:
      - No literal translation of English tech idioms — the main source of garbled Russian. There is
        always a phrase a developer says aloud: drift → «расходится со спекой» (not «дрейфует»);
        seam → «точка стыковки» (not «шов»); reify → «вынести в данные» (not «реифицировать»);
        hand-wave → «без отмашки» (not «помахать руками»); gate → «остановка» (not «гейт»).
      - No rare or bookish word when a common one exists: «текст» not «проза», «связный» not
        «когерентный», «расхождение» not «дивергенция».
      - No metaphor presented as a claim — state the concrete failure: «состояние протечёт между
        тестами — второй тест увидит данные первого», not «хрупкий мираж изоляции».
      - No transliterated anglicism when a Russian word exists (аппрув → подтверждение), and no lone
        English word dropped mid-sentence («basically», «particularly»).
      - No ornamental adjectives, no aphorisms.
      - Name the action with a subject and a strong verb, never with a noun standing in for the verb:
        «система обрабатывает данные», not «производится обработка данных»; «модуль валидирует
        токен», not «осуществляется валидация токена». Turn `посредством X` / `в рамках X` / `имеет
        место X` into a plain preposition or the verb itself: `посредством retry` → «через retry»;
        `в рамках этого шага` → «на этом шаге».

      Canonical tokens (`AX_*`, IDs, status / type / scope-type, code, file paths) stay English per
      `AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE`, shown as a tag beside the plain sentence — never as the sentence itself.

      Test before sending: read it as a busy mid-level developer. Stumbled on a word, or it sounds
      like a blog showing off? Rewrite plainer. Would you say it aloud to a colleague at the next desk?

      - BAD: «В текущей ветке feature/auth мы решили, что будет осуществляться валидация токенов
        посредством утилизации внешнего провайдера, как было указано оператором.»
      - GOOD: «Модуль валидирует токены через внешнего провайдера.»
      - Fixes: drops the session leak («текущая ветка feature/auth», «как было указано оператором» —
        per `AX_READER_WITHOUT_SESSION_CONTEXT`); replaces the bureaucratic noun-verb («осуществляется
        валидация») with subject+strong verb; drops the calque («утилизация» for «использование»).
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION" cross-cutting="true">
      The operator never sees the agent's machinery. No «DIRECTIVE ACTIVATED», no mode-detection
      narration, no «grounding collected», no «перехожу к STEP_2», no frame-stack talk. Detection,
      reading, and step transitions happen silently. Mode / scope / confidence live in the dim service
      line and the log, nowhere else.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_OPERATOR_OUTPUT_LIVE_TEXT" cross-cutting="true">
      Everything the operator reads is a normal chat message with live-rendered markdown — headers,
      bold, lists, blockquotes, tables. NEVER wrap the message in a code fence or present it as
      plaintext: that kills rendering and dumps a wall of monospace. Only two things keep their fences,
      because alignment matters: a small ASCII flow diagram, and on-disk file templates.
    </Axiom>

















































    <Axiom id="AX_CAP_5">Five critic results is the hard automatic cap. At the fifth result — regardless
      of sensor verdict — the operator must dispose the cycle before readiness or another dispatch. If that
      round made no edits, the choices are `CLEAN`, `CONTINUE THROUGH ROUND N` with an explicit higher cap,
      or `RESTART: reason`; if it made edits, CLEAN is unavailable because the changed bytes have not been
      reviewed, so only CONTINUE or RESTART are offered. A raised cap behaves identically when reached. The
    sensor cannot authorize continuation, and continuation is never readiness.</Axiom>
  </BeliefState>

  <SessionState>
    <Contract id="SESSION_FILE_FORMAT">
      A single temporary scratch file for the current SDD session — `specs/.sdd-session.md`, gitignored, not
      a deliverable. It lets a multi-step or multi-scope flow survive a context squeeze or a session break
      within the same piece of work. The kernel reads it on entry and updates it after each loaded
      directive. Its lifetime is controlled by one typed terminal result:

      `TerminalDecision = continue | pause | complete`.

      - `continue` means a known next link remains in the current chain — root → scope → module → integrated
        review → scaffold → execute, including another scope in dependency order. Preserve the same session;
        an internal handoff never closes it.
      - `pause` means an Approval Check, external-review wait, critic/scaffold cap, blocker, failed check, or
        operator choice to resume later. Preserve the same session and its working set; a pause is not
        completion.
      - `complete` is legal only after the current terminal menu records an explicit operator choice named
        `complete` or `finish` and the owning step has established `unfinished chained work: none`. That step
        runs exact `npx gennady sdd-session close` once. No inferred completion, successful intermediate
        artifact, merge, ticket close, empty subagent queue, or context end may call it. `close` removes only
        the scratch file; the durable `.gitignore` entry remains.

      A live session is never silently reused for an unrelated incoming intent. The already-gathered
      `sdd-state` `[SESSION]` block is the current-session card. This compatibility decision is the first
      write barrier: before readiness, migration, glossary, journal, spec, ticket, config, or any other
      project mutation, compare its literal
      `intent`, `scale`, `working set`, and `open` fields with the incoming intent. If related continuation
      cannot be proved, show that card and ask exactly one operator decision: `continue existing` or
      `close-and-start-new` with the proposed new intent and, only for a SCALE-consuming authoring owner,
      the proposed scale shown. Preserve the old file while the
      answer is pending (`pause`). `continue existing` routes from the stored fields without calling `open`.
      `close-and-start-new` itself confirms the displayed new values: run exact
      `npx gennady sdd-session close`; only after success run exact
      `npx gennady sdd-session open --intent <confirmed-intent> --scale <confirmed-scale>` for a
      SCALE-consuming authoring owner, or exact `npx gennady sdd-session open --intent <confirmed-intent>`
      otherwise. Never call `--help`, guess values, or use
      `sdd-session open` idempotence as evidence that an existing intent is compatible. A confirmed
      same-chain stage transition may use exact `npx gennady sdd-session set intent "<confirmed-intent>"`
      and `npx gennady sdd-session set scale "<confirmed-scale>"`; it is not a way to relabel unrelated
      work.

      The `scale:` field carries operator-confirmed SCALE (`AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH`) for authoring
      routes whose depth actually changes: `project-setup`, `new-scope`, `evolve-scope`,
      `module-decomposition`, and `multi-scope`, routed to root/scope/module/infra/interface. Those routes
      confirm SCALE once and record it in the same `sdd-session open --intent ... --scale ...` call.
      `execute`, `recover-from-code`, `scaffold`, `reconcile`, and `critic` consume no SCALE: the router
      asks no SCALE question and opens a new session with intent only. A compatible existing session may
      retain its already-recorded scale untouched; non-consumers do not reinterpret it.

      The `glossary:` section is the session's terminology anchor (`AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE`). Only the
      stateful orchestrator that has crossed the compatibility/open barrier may mutate it. For each
      accepted term, write exactly `<term> — <definition>` to the regular non-symlink payload
      `.claude/tmp/sdd-glossary-term.txt`, then run the router's exact glossaryUpdate ToolCall; the
      successful tool consumes that payload. Never pass agent-produced glossary text inline. A pre-session router buffers a
      suggestion without writing it. Read-only critic/audit/code-review workers return
      `GlossarySuggestions` and never invoke this ToolCall. In compact audit/code-review output, the final
      `@glossary suggestions=[<term> — <definition>; ...]` line is the serialization of that exact field:
      `[]` maps to no suggestions; each semicolon-delimited item maps byte-for-byte to one
      `GlossarySuggestions` item. The compatible stateful orchestrator deduplicates these against the
      saved glossary, presents genuinely new/conflicting terms at its next existing operator checkpoint
      (never opens a glossary-only Ask), and persists only accepted items through `glossaryUpdate`.
      Every worker dispatch receives the current saved glossary as read-only input; a direct read-only
      skill reads only the `glossary:` block from `specs/.sdd-session.md` when that file exists, otherwise
      uses an empty list. Every later mention reuses the recorded name
      verbatim; before introducing a new name, check this list in the saved `sdd-state` snapshot.

      Router preflight runs only AFTER compatibility/open. Its readiness cases consume the existing
      `activeRouterState` without invoking a readiness mutation flow. Migration alone returns exactly one
      alias `migrationState`; the router replaces its routing input with that snapshot and never refreshes
      it itself. A branch
      journal entry is written through the already-compatible session, so no pre-session journal buffer
      exists and no write can land in an unrelated session.

      ```markdown
      # SDD session — <YYYY-MM-DD>
      intent: <classified intent>
      scale: <product | library | module | function | fix> — operator-confirmed
      working set:
        - <scope / module spec path> — <why touched> — <status>
      glossary:
        - <term> — <definition>
      journal:
        - <step / loaded directive> → <what it produced>
      open: <unresolved decisions / questions>
      ```
    </Contract>


  </SessionState>

  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_SPECS` | `specs/README.md` absent, or no approved scope spec to scaffold |
    | `H_SCOPE_NOT_DECOMPOSED` | Scope type is missing/ambiguous; product/library Module Map is not a non-empty exact manifest of its canonical module specs; or an interface is used as a task owner. Start the nested same-chain module-decomposition correction below, preserve `intent: scaffold` and the exact bounded target-set, then return to scaffold `STEP_0_INTAKE`; infrastructure is the sole flat-scope exception. |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` | Tasks exist + mode verb absent (per `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`) |
    | `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE` | A rule reference or `<DependsOn>` entry does not resolve to an existing file (`AX_RULES_RESOLUTION_HARD_FAIL`) |
    | `H_RULES_CYCLE` | `<DependsOn>` closure cycles during per-phase rule activation (STEP_3) |
    | `H_VERIFICATION_COMMAND_MISSING` | A `<RequiresVerification>` alias is absent from infra-spec Verification Commands (alias, ticket ID) |
    | `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING` | The target scope being scaffolded (the intake target) lacks a Bootstrap Requirements section — checked ONLY for that scope, not for every ✅ scope in the Portal (did not pass the scope flow) |
    | `H_SPEC_SCHEMA_INVALID` | `sdd-state` reports the target scope/module spec as `invalid`; structural evidence is ambiguous and only its owning authoring flow may repair it |
    | `H_ID_COLLISION` | A generated Task-ID / slug collides with an existing one |
    | `H_TYPING_SCENARIO_MISSING` | A DbC contract in Spec References has no `contract`-level typing scenario mapped to a test |
    | `H_TICKET_AUTHORING_INVALID` | One just-filled ticket still fails its bounded single-ticket authoring gate after three repair attempts; no later ticket or index may be created |
    | `H_SCAFFOLD_NOT_EXECUTABLE` | The feasibility critic cannot reach an operator-disposed state within its bounded loop, or a required fresh fallback cannot receive the full latest target-set plus retained context/history |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected the breakdown or the test plan |
  </HaltConditions>

